.TH GUIMOD 1
.SH NAME
guimod \- send a desktop module's record (tile, status, notification)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBguimod tile\fR \fIname\fR \fItext\fR ...
\fBguimod status\fR \fIname\fR \fItext\fR ...
\fBguimod notify\fR \fIname\fR \fItext\fR ...
\fBguimod close\fR \fIname\fR
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Guimod
writes one record to the desktop's FIFO
.BR /var/run/gui ,
which the launcher reads (Galtzagorriak MINIX, spec K.3, K.6).
A
.I module
is the caller's real uid plus
.I name
(1 to 10 characters): it can change only its own entries.
Its process is
.BR guimod 's
parent, usually the shell running the module script; when that process
exits or is killed, its tile and status go.
.TP
.B tile
sets or changes the module's tile, one row on the launcher's HOME page
(at most 31 characters are shown).
.TP
.B status
sets the module's short text on the status line (the title bar), at most
6 characters.
.TP
.B notify
counts a notification (the unread count on the status line) and shows
its text on the message line for 2 seconds.
.TP
.B close
removes the module's tile and status.
.PP
The words of
.I text
are joined with spaces, as by
.BR echo .
A record is at most 128 bytes.
.B Guimod
never waits: with no desktop running, or a full FIFO while a full-screen
app runs, the record is dropped.
.PP
.B Guimod
is set-uid root only to write the real uid into the record (a FIFO does
not say who wrote into it); it drops root right after opening the FIFO.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
0 sent, 1 no desktop or the desktop is busy, 2 usage.
.SH EXAMPLE
A battery tile in three lines
.RB ( /usr/share/examples/gui/battile.sh ,
package gmdemo):
.PP
.nf
IFS='= '
while read key soc rest </dev/power
do guimod tile battery "Battery $soc%"; sleep 60; done
.fi
.SH FILES
/var/run/gui	the desktop's FIFO (root, mode 0620, group gui)
.SH "SEE ALSO"
The manual chapter "The launcher" (docs/manual/launcher.md), the
Development Guide chapter 7.
